+### Local SM8250 preparation
+
+The local TB-J716F adaptation checkout, `work/ShengUfs-sm8250`, adds
+host-testable helpers and a restricted command path, with board adapters in the
+device repository. They are not selected by `NativeUfs` or the flashed TB-J716F
+boot path. The independent `j716f-host-ufs-adapter` target has completed the
+official kernel build and static verification described below.
+The `UfsPlatform.h` / `UfsObservation.h`
+observer requires an exact SM8250 identity and established
+read-access grant, checks power/reset/bus clocks, and reads only a fixed set of
+GCC/HCI/SMMU-ID registers. It does not follow firmware list pointers or issue
+register writes, UIC commands or DMA. Expected SIDs are source facts, not a
+measurement of firmware ownership.
+
+The separate grouped planner requires a complete validated stream/context
+snapshot and an explicit common owner grant. It checks both SIDs together and
+revalidates the captured snapshot; it supplies no actual lock or MMIO commit.
+Its `planInto` entry uses caller-retained output storage instead of a full
+`Plan` stack temporary. It rejects overlapping inputs without changing the
+output, so callers must check the returned error. The board handoff transaction
+retains separate planning and revalidation workspaces. The value-returning
+`plan` and the `revalidate` helper, which uses a full local plan, remain available
+for host consumers and are not used by this transaction.
+
+`ReadCommandPolicy.h` validates the final request packet and PRD against a
+five-command allowlist: NOP, the power-mode attribute read, TEST UNIT READY,
+READ CAPACITY(10), and READ(10). It requires the fixed one-slot DMA layout and
+exact tag, LUN, direction and transfer length. READ(10) is limited to one
+4 KiB block from an explicit known-block allowlist after capacity discovery;
+writes, arbitrary queries and other SCSI commands are rejected. Response
+validation checks OCS, response type, tag/LUN, status, residual and sense data.
+The policy itself allocates nothing and performs no MMIO or DMA.
+
+`RestrictedUfs.h` supplies a non-copyable single-slot state machine. It enforces
+NOP → power-mode check → TEST UNIT READY → READ CAPACITY(10) → allowed reads,
+retains the accepted request and context, and calls the shared owner's readiness
+and DMA gates before submission. Polling has both a time limit and a poll-count
+limit. At most two retries are allowed, only after a validated completed SCSI
+BUSY or TASK SET FULL response; all attempts share the original budget.
+Doorbell zero alone is not completion. Ambiguous prepare/acknowledge/ring
+failures, in-flight timeouts and invalid completions enter quarantine and retain
+the resource claim. A software halt is not proof that the hardware queue stopped.
+The board adapter described below implements the `Port` callbacks for MMIO,
+cache maintenance and owned-buffer preparation. Its host-model verification
+does not establish a working hardware transport.
+
+The board-side `socs/sm8250/kits/Sm8250Platform/AppsSmmuOwner.h` and
+`AppsSmmuOwner.cpp` define 13 C ABI functions backed by one implementation
+translation unit. The software owner provides bounded, nonblocking metadata
+and transaction gates, exact resource claims, monotonic lease tokens, and
+publication/queue/DMA lifecycle checks without allocation or MMIO. Acquisition
+requires an explicitly installed board handoff with a nonzero authorization
+reference and attestations of valid exclusive authority, a stopped queue and
+idle DMA for the exact controller/SMMU/SID/slot/bank/DMA tuple. The owner cannot
+establish those hardware facts or infer firmware/TZ ownership from unused
+contexts. The independent target links exactly one implementation; later
+integrations must preserve that requirement.
+
+Release requires a stopped queue, known-idle DMA and no partial publication.
+A published mapping retains its claim for the same tuple to reacquire; only an
+explicitly confirmed unpublish permits removing it. Quarantine releases the CPU
+transaction lease but permanently retains the resource claim until reboot or
+platform shutdown. There is no recovery/reset API. Tokens are capabilities for
+trusted callers and must not be shared concurrently. If quarantine encounters
+metadata contention or a stale token, the state machine remains terminal and
+does not free the existing resources or lease.
+
+The existing `NativeUfs.cpp` command path and `AppsSmmuLock.h` are unchanged and
+do not use this owner or policy. The observer and grouped planner remain pure
+helpers; ownership and hardware publication belong to the separate board
+transaction. No `--observer` or `--platform` build option exists.
+
+From the adaptation workspace root on the Linux build host, use a manifest
+pinning the source, owner, runner and prepared ABI inputs, with fresh output
+directories:
+
+```sh
+python3 analysis/tools/check_shengufs_restricted.py \
+  --workspace "$PWD" --manifest /path/to/input-manifest.json \
+  --output /path/to/fresh-host-check
+python3 analysis/tools/build_shengufs_restricted.py \
+  --workspace "$PWD" --manifest /path/to/input-manifest.json \
+  --output /path/to/fresh-arm-check
+```
+
+The host runner covers the policy, state machine and multi-TU/thread owner tests
+with ASan/UBSan, plus the preceding observer/planner and root-path regressions.
+The ARM runner builds the official `build-arm-driver.py ShengUfs` archive and
+separate ABI bridge/owner objects, then checks a relocatable link and all 13
+owner symbols. Each run records its input manifest and verification results.
+An object link is not a kernel link, and none of these checks
+qualifies register access, DMA, block reads or root mounting on TB-J716F.
+
+#### Board adapter and current verification
+
+The board's `Sm8250Platform` now implements `Sm8250UfsHandoff`, `Sm8250UfsHci`
+and `Sm8250UfsRuntime`. Handoff requires actual register-access, SMMU-visibility,
+exclusive-authority and DMA evidence, including exclusion of external writers
+and proof that old transactions are drained. It captures and revalidates the
+complete visible stream/context set, publishes the granted dual-SID routes,
+and leaves the already stopped host queue stopped. It neither stops unknown
+firmware queues nor performs cold initialization, PHY/ICE/TCU, UIC or global
+SMMU programming.
+
+The HCI adapter installs one owned request list, submits only slot 0, and
+implements descriptor preparation, bounded polling, response checks and cache
+maintenance for the restricted command chain. Runtime provides the IOKit
+contiguous allocation, register mappings and ARM64 cache/barrier operations.
+Its startup entry only marks registration; trusted platform calls must separately
+authorize allocation and activation with actual handoff evidence. Published or
+quarantined DMA and mappings remain retained even after a confirmed queue stop.
+
+Stock TB-J716F ZUI 15.0.485 DTB0/DTBO1 resources have been independently checked
+against the original images. Expected SIDs and GCC branch offsets remain Linux
+source references. These inputs do not prove live register accessibility,
+firmware/TZ ownership or the required handoff facts.
+
+The 2026-11-13 ASan/UBSan host run passed 28 HCI scenarios and 148 callback
+failures, 67 handoff scenarios and 500 callback failures, and 11 integrated
+pipeline scenarios, plus prior regressions. The pipeline combines the actual
+handoff, HCI, command-state and owner modules with modeled MMIO and separate
+CPU/device DMA views; it does not insert synthetic owner-ready transitions.
+The workspace runner is `analysis/tools/check_sm8250_ufs_adapter.py`, and this
+run is recorded in `analysis/ufs-adapter-20261113/host-result.json` with input
+hashes. It does not execute the IOKit Runtime, access hardware or use QEMU.
+
+The independent target completed the official kernel bootstrap, nine archive
+recipes, native kernel link and ordinary ARM64 prelink. The build and static
+acceptance reports are `analysis/host-ufs-adapter-20261113/kernel-03.json` and
+`verification-03.json`. All 20 owner/Runtime exports are unique in the native
+and prelinked outputs, with their code preserved. Machine-code checks confirm
+that the startup entry only stores its registration flag, without MMIO or
+allocator calls. The maximum analyzed internal call chain is 5376 bytes of
+stack, including conservative internal callback sets. External XNU/IOKit frames
+and interrupt nesting are excluded, so this is not a bound on the complete
+running kernel stack.
+
+Build and static acceptance do not establish runtime execution. The adapter
+is not enabled on the tablet; the production archive recipe, root publication
+and persistent storage path remain unchanged. Actual TB-J716F register access,
+DMA, known-block reads, IOStorage/root integration and iPadOS userland are still
+unqualified. All 14 device porting areas, including storage, remain `planned`
+pending the corresponding hardware evidence.
+
